    "notes": "Reviewed source mapping. Basis: NJ FAIR Act, P.L. 2026, c.43 (A3497 1R), §§3-6, 9. All five state rules are not_yet_effective, with effect on 2027-07-01 computed from §9 (first day of the twelfth month after enactment on July 20, 2026). This is a potential overlap flag only, not a finding that any municipal ordinance is invalid. State requirements: §4(a) bars owners from paying for or using a coordinator's services. §4(b)-(e) bar coordinators from facilitating restraints on competition, parallel pricing coordination, and performing a coordinating function. §6(b) bars municipalities from enacting an ordinance that conflicts with the act, except ordinances 'explicitly authorized or required by any other law'. §6(a) and §5 make the act additive to the NJ Antitrust Act (no authorization of conduct unlawful under it). Potentially overlapping municipal bans: Hoboken § 158-2 (bars algorithmic price fixing using nonpublic competitor information under 365 days old; fine up to $2,000 and/or 90 days' community service), and Jersey City § 218-12(2)(a)-(b) (bars lessors from subscribing to, paying for or 'otherwise utilizing' a Service Provider, and bars Service Providers from facilitating agreements not to compete). Carveouts: FAIR excludes government affordability controls and, from 'coordinating function', research, free public rent estimates and equal-terms brokerage databases. Jersey City exempts licensed real estate agents and same-owner multi-property actions. Hoboken excludes publicly available or 365+ day-old data. Possible differences to review include Jersey City's 'otherwise utilize' wording, the differing definitions of nonpublic information, and the ordinances' own penalty and enforcement schemes, since the municipal rules are in force now while FAIR is not yet effective. Hoboken's and Jersey City's ordinances are municipal-level law, not shown to be 'authorized or required by any other law' in the evidence supplied. Unknown coverage addresses: 140. "